===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|gaudi}}, from {{der|en|fro|gaudie}}, from {{der|en|ML.|gaudia}}. equivalent to {{suffix|en|gaud|t1=ornament, trinket|y}}.

Alternatively, from {{inh|en|enm|gaudi}}, {{m|enm|gawdy|t=yellowish}}, from {{der|en|fro|gaude}}, {{m|fro|galde|t=weld (the plant)}}, from {{der|en|frk|*walda}}, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*walþō}}, {{m|gem-pro|*walþijō}}, akin to {{cog|ang|*weald}}, {{m|ang|*wielde}} (>{{cog|enm|welde}}, {{m|enm|wolde}} and Anglo-{{cog|la|walda|t=alum}}), {{cog|gml|wolde}}, {{cog|dum|woude}}. More at {{cog|en|weld}}.

A common claim that the word derives from [[w:Antoni Gaudí|Antoni Gaudí]], designer of Barcelona's [[w:Sagrada Família|Sagrada Família Basilica]], is incorrect: the word was in use centuries before Gaudí was born.

====Adjective====
{{en-adj|er}}

# Very [[showy]] or [[ornamented]], now especially when [[excessive]], or in a [[tasteless]] or [[vulgar]] manner.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Hamlet|act=I|scene=iii|passage=Costly thy habit as thy purse can buy, / But not expressed in fancy; rich, not '''gaudy'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Cibber Refusal|page=2|passage=Though, I confeſs, ''Paris'' has its Charms; but to me they are like thoſe of a Coquette, gay and '''gavvdy'''; they ſerve to amuſe vvith, but a Man vvould not chuſe to be marry'd to them.}}
#* {{RQ:Austen Pride and Prejudice|text=The rooms were lofty and handsome, and their furniture suitable to the fortune of its proprietor; but Elizabeth saw, with admiration of his taste, that it was neither '''gaudy''' nor uselessly fine; with less of splendour, and more real elegance, than the furniture of Rosings.}}
#* {{RQ:Dickens Martin Chuzzlewit|chapter=3|page=19|passage=A faded, and an ancient dragon he was; and many a wintry storm of rain, snow, sleet, and hail, had changed his colour from a '''gaudy''' blue to a faint lack-lustre shade of gray.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1887|author=Homer Greene|title=Burnham Breaker|passage=A large '''gaudy''', flowing cravat, and an ill-used silk hat, set well back on the wearer's head, completed this somewhat noticeable costume.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Thomas Hauser; Marilyn Cole Lownes|title=How Bling-bling Took Over the Ring|journal=The Observer|date=9 January 2005
|passage='''Gaudy''' jewellery might offend some people's sense of style. But former heavyweight champion and grilling-machine entrepreneur George Foreman is philosophical about today's craze for bling-bling.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} {{cap|fun}}; [[merry]]; [[festive]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Antony and Cleopatra|III|xiii|passage=Let's have one other '''gaudy''' night.}}
#* {{RQ:Tennyson Idylls|passage=And for my strange petition I will make <br>Amends hereafter by some '''gaudy''' day}}
#* {{RQ:Twain Huckleberry Finn|22|passage=And then, there he was, slim and handsome, and dressed the '''gaudiest''' and prettiest you ever saw...}}

